          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly avows Goneril’s loving emotional state toward Lear. The continuation amplifies that claimed love as immeasurable, so reporting or presenting love as true is its clear dominant mode, regardless of sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The occurrence does not create, renew, or affirm a commitment to perform filial duties. Goneril compares her love with that of a child, but she claims an emotional magnitude rather than positioning herself as bound by an obligation. Lear’s grant follows as the reward in his love contest, not as acceptance of a promise made by the focal expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is a composed, extended answer to Lear’s formal solicitation, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Its hyperbole and emotional vocabulary do not by themselves supply expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\\n\\nGONERIL.\\nSir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression functions contextually as a competitive answer and an indirect claim to merit Lear’s largest bounty. This strategic bidding or flattering function is moderately supported, though it remains dependent on the explicitly announced contest rather than being encoded in the words alone.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public court ceremony, Lear asks his daughters to declare which loves him most and explicitly links their answers to the size of their territorial awards. Goneril, speaking first, responds with an elaborate and hyperbolic profession of filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No distinct undertaking is established by Goneril’s focal expression or its continuation: she professes surpassing filial love but does not promise conduct or affirm a specified duty. Lear immediately grants her territory, which constitutes uptake of her answer within the love contest, not acceptance of a commitment. The later supplied passages describing wrongs and harms by Lear’s elder daughters undermine the profession’s sincerity, but they do not alter its original avowal force or establish that it was a promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognised as William Shakespeare’s King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly establishes the public, competitive setting and later counterevidence to Goneril’s profession, but it does not directly disclose her inward emotional state at the moment of speaking. The supplied excerpts also do not warrant conclusions about every event between the declaration and the later scenes.\"}"
